        "finalAnswer": "**Six retainers a year — on purpose.**\n\nHere's the thinking behind it:\n\n- **Quality over volume.** Past six concurrent retainers, founder time per client drops below the threshold that makes Foundry's work distinguishable from a standard growth shop. Six is where the work stays exceptional.\n- **The economics work because of the \"no.\"** Foundry's pricing and positioning depend on selectivity. Saying yes to a seventh client isn't a win — it's a dilution.\n- **It's a hard cap, not an aspiration.** Owen tracks the count. The partners say the no. The seventh prospect gets declined regardless of how attractive the opportunity looks.\n\nThe qualifying rubric is tight too: brand AND growth on the same retainer, founder available 2 hours per week minimum, pricing floor cleared. If any of those conditions don't hold, it's a pass.\n\nThe underlying belief, as the founder put it: *\"Pricing — we pick six clients a year. Channel — we own the loop, not a channel. Boredom — we refuse work that bores us, even at scale.\"*\n\nThat last part matters. The boredom test is what most studios fail. Foundry writes it into the principles before signing.",
